module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_drops_rederivation_instrument_sits_in_the_population_it_measures

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_drops_rederivation_instrument_sits_in_the_population_it_measures: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_drops_rederivation_instrument_sits_in_the_population_it_measures" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a declared drop is bounded by a census, and the census is itself a module of the population the drop covers, so the population can break the instrument that would count it and nothing goes red.** INVALID STATE: gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn names its population as what gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:required-lane-resolution-census enumerates, and that instrument's entry (gunbc.required_lane_resolution_census_live) imports modules that are outside the two emitted closures, so a refusal in any of them stops the instrument at resolve before it prints a single module. The drop's re-derivation then answers nothing, while the drop still reads as bounded.",

    "HARM. The drop becomes unbounded with nothing red. The required job v2-bootstrap judges only the two emitted closures, so it stays green when the census's own closure stops resolving; the census is not a required context, so its failure is a dispatch-time exit and nobody is looking. A reader who cites the drop as a bounded population is citing a number no instrument can currently produce.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS, both measured by executed runs of the dispatched instrument (instrument-dispatch, label //gunbc/instruments:required-lane-resolution-census, exit 1 both times, no module list printed). At main 0ac3102d1b07 (run 39387690147): resolve failed for dag/gunbc/required_lane_resolution_census_live.dag at dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag:2989 with call shape mismatch calling updated, argument on and argument note each supplied more than once. At main 585d918af6c3 (run 39389715573), after that line was repaired: dag/extdeps/github/expressions.dag:702:9 non-exhaustive match, missing variant LogicalNot, and dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_event_carrier.dag:444:117 type mismatch, expected Int, got RoadmapEventId. Over the same interval the required job v2-bootstrap succeeded at 585d918af6c3 (run 39385184965): it records each emitted closure only as a closure_identity hash and an emitted file count, not as a module list, so the closures the drop is defined against exist only inside the job. This differs from a_census_is_cited_wider_than_the_closure_its_instrument_compiles, whose reading is present and correct but cited too wide, and from an_instrument_resolves_wider_than_the_subject_it_measures, whose instrument dies on cost; here the instrument is refused on correctness, by modules it was supposed to be counting.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACT, closure record: the required job records each emitted closure only as a closure_identity hash plus an emitted file count (v2-bootstrap run 39385184965: self-host 4e519e8f, 249 files; v2-native-cli 6478154a, 240 files; no artifact), never the module list. The drop's coverage therefore cannot be checked from the job's own record: whether the census closure lies inside either emitted closure is not decidable without re-deriving the closures by a separate run.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. The break is found only when someone dispatches the census by hand. CEILING: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, and this row said structurally guaranteed until the parent rejected that wording at head 20a75dc4cdc. Mechanical prevention here is two obligations, both open and neither waiting on anybody: (1) some REQUIRED context must call the join that already exists, run_rostered_row_join (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/rostered_row_join.rs:599), and refuse on a non-empty JoinReport, so an unrostered or unbacked row fails a job rather than failing only a hand run; (2) the census this drop is bounded by must be judged on that required path, so a refusal anywhere in its closure fails a required context instead of surfacing as a dispatch-time exit nobody reads. Structural impossibility really is out of reach while the drop's population is deliberately larger than what the required job compiles, because that gap IS the drop; that is not a licence to claim structural now, because (1) needs no new capability at all, only a caller.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS AN OBLIGATION AND ITS MISSING ENROLLER: the missing enroller is a required-context CALLER of run_rostered_row_join. Measured at this head: grep -rn 'run_rostered_row_join(' --include=*.rs over the checkout returns EXACTLY ONE line, src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/rostered_row_join.rs:599, and that line is the DEFINITION (pub fn run_rostered_row_join(index: &DeclarationIndex) -> Result<JoinReport, String>). A call cannot be spelled without the open paren, so that single hit IS the absence of a caller: the function is never called, in production or in its own test module. The only other mentions anywhere in the checkout are prose. No workflow dispatches it, no instrument_targets entry reaches it, no shipped claim imports it, and the module's own test module exercises the inner file-to-roster arms rather than this entry point. So the sentence at dag/gunbc/derived_row_roster_seed_growth.dag:74 that the file-versus-roster join arms 'execute inside run_rostered_row_join on the required parse path (witnesses lane)' states a consumer that is absent from the tree, and it is the sentence that let the hole read as covered. The arms have run only inside a claim nimble-badger-800 wrote for itself and did not ship. THE TRIGGER IS THEREFORE THE ENROLLER ITSELF, not a capability to be delivered later: wire run_rostered_row_join into a required context and let a DeclaredNotRostered or RosteredNotDeclared finding fail that job. The mutation proving the hole is open is nimble-badger-800's, reproduced in PR comment 6289242275: renaming only the identity field of THIS row compiles clean (its own words: COMPILE[renamed] rc=0 outputs=23, '0 blocking error(s)') and nothing shipped goes red, which is the row describing its own blind spot. Charter 6 (the native compiler judging the withdrawn population) remains the trigger for the SECOND obligation, the census being judged natively on the required path; it is explicitly NOT a prerequisite for the first. NOT satisfied by an author re-running the census, or the join, by hand after each break: that is the mode that let two SHAs pass unobserved.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
